## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to model DP with obstacles and variable step sizes, requiring careful base cases and transition logic. It also assesses both memoization and tabulation familiarity.

## Key Concepts

- DP with multiple transitions (1..k)
- Handling blocked steps
- Memoization vs tabulation
- Time complexity: O(n*maxJump); Space: O(n) (or O(maxJump) with optimization)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Bottom-up tabulation
function countWays(n, maxJump, blocked) {
  const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    if (blocked[i]) { dp[i] = 0; continue; }
    for (let j = 1; j <= maxJump; j++) {
      if (i - j >= 0) dp[i] += dp[i - j];
    }
  }
  return dp[n];
}
```

```javascript
// Memoized recursive version
function countWaysRec(n, maxJump, blocked, i = n, memo = {}) {
  if (i === 0) return 1;
  if (i < 0 || blocked[i]) return 0;
  if (memo[i] !== undefined) return memo[i];
  let ways = 0;
  for (let t = 1; t <= maxJump; t++) ways += countWaysRec(n, maxJump, blocked, i - t, memo);
  memo[i] = ways;
  return ways;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize to O(maxJump) space with a sliding window?
- How would you adjust for modulo arithmetic to prevent overflow for large n?

## Why Asked
Tests understanding of graph traversal fundamentals, algorithm selection, and space-time tradeoffs in real-world scenarios.

## Key Concepts
**BFS (Breadth-First Search)**: Uses queue, explores neighbors level-by-level. Time: O(V+E). Space: O(V) worst case (all nodes in queue). Guarantees shortest path in unweighted graphs.

**DFS (Depth-First Search)**: Uses stack or recursion, explores as deep as possible before backtracking. Time: O(V+E). Space: O(h) where h is maximum depth (recursion stack), O(V) worst case for skewed graphs.

## When to Use Each
**BFS**: Shortest path routing, social network friend suggestions, web crawlers finding closest pages, level-order tree traversals.

**DFS**: Topological sorting, cycle detection, maze solving, finding connected components, backtracking problems like Sudoku or N-Queens.

## Code Example
```javascript
// BFS - finds shortest path
function bfs(graph, start) {
  const queue = [start];
  const visited = new Set([start]);
  const distances = { [start]: 0 };

  while (queue.length) {
    const node = queue.shift();
    for (const neighbor of graph[node]) {
      if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {
        visited.add(neighbor);
        distances[neighbor] = distances[node] + 1;
        queue.push(neighbor);
      }
    }
  }
  return distances;
}

// DFS - recursive implementation
function dfs(graph, node, visited = new Set(), result = []) {
  if (visited.has(node)) return result;

  visited.add(node);
  result.push(node);

  for (const neighbor of graph[node]) {
    dfs(graph, neighbor, visited, result);
  }

  return result;
}

// DFS - iterative implementation
function dfsIterative(graph, start) {
  const stack = [start];
  const visited = new Set();
  const result = [];

  while (stack.length) {
    const node = stack.pop();
    if (visited.has(node)) continue;

    visited.add(node);
    result.push(node);

    // Add neighbors to stack (reverse for consistent order)
    for (let i = graph[node].length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
      const neighbor = graph[node][i];
      if (!visited.has(neighbor)) {
        stack.push(neighbor);
      }
    }
  }
  return result;
}
```

## Memory Considerations
BFS can consume significant memory for wide graphs (queue holds all nodes at current level). DFS is memory-efficient for deep, narrow graphs but may cause stack overflow for very deep recursion. Choose based on graph structure and problem requirements.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to frame a sign-assignment problem as a DP and implement both memoization and tabulation, a common interview pattern.

## Key Concepts
- DP state reduction to subset sum
- Space optimization with a 1D array
- Memoization vs bottom-up trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tabulation and Memo versions
function countTargetSumTab(nums, S) {
  const total = nums.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0);
  if (Math.abs(S) > total || (total+S)%2 !== 0) return 0;
  const target = (total+S)/2;
  const dp = Array(target+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (const n of nums) {
    for (let t = target; t >= n; t--) dp[t] += dp[t-n];
  }
  return dp[target];
}

function countTargetSumMemo(nums, S) {
  const total = nums.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0);
  if (Math.abs(S) > total || (total+S)%2 !== 0) return 0;
  const target = (total+S)/2;
  const memo = new Map();
  function dfs(i, sum){
    if (sum > target) return 0;
    if (i === nums.length) return sum === target ? 1 : 0;
    const key = i+':'+sum;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    const res = dfs(i+1, sum+nums[i]) + dfs(i+1, sum);
    memo.set(key, res);
    return res;
  }
  return dfs(0,0);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to translate dependency constraints into a topological order and compute earliest finish times via DP. It also touches complexity and edge cases (cycles).

## Key Concepts

- Topological sort (Kahn or DFS)
- DAG longest path / critical path
- Dynamic programming on DAG
- Time and space complexity

## Code Example

```javascript
function computeProject(durations, edges) {
  const n = durations.length;
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v] of edges) { adj[u].push(v); indeg[v]++; }
  const est = Array(n).fill(0);
  const finish = Array(n).fill(0);
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  let idx = 0;
  while (idx < q.length) {
    const u = q[idx++];
    finish[u] = est[u] + durations[u];
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
      est[v] = Math.max(est[v], finish[u]);
      if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
    }
  }
  const project = Math.max(...finish);
  return { finish, project };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect cycles if the DAG assumption fails? 
- How would you adapt for tasks with minimum and maximum parallelism constraints?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests your ability to implement a Trie with efficient prefix queries, which is fundamental for autocomplete features. The per-node counter design enables O(k) prefix counting where k is the prefix length, and O(m) insertion where m is the word length.

## Key Concepts

- Trie structure with per-node subtree counts
- Incrementing counts during insertion for O(1) prefix queries
- Prefix traversal and counting
- Edge case handling for empty strings, null inputs, and non-existent prefixes

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.subtreeCount = 0;
    this.isWord = false;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() {
    this.root = new TrieNode();
  }

  insert(word) {
    if (!word || typeof word !== 'string') {
      return;
    }

    let node = this.root;
    node.subtreeCount++;

    for (const char of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        node.children.set(char, new TrieNode());
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
      node.subtreeCount++;
    }

    node.isWord = true;
  }

  getPrefixCount(prefix) {
    if (!prefix || typeof prefix !== 'string') {
      return 0;
    }

    let node = this.root;

    for (const char of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(char)) {
        return 0;
      }
      node = node.children.get(char);
    }

    return node.subtreeCount;
  }
}

// Example usage:
const trie = new Trie();
trie.insert('cat');
trie.insert('cater');
trie.insert('category');
trie.insert('dog');

console.log(trie.getPrefixCount('cat'));  // 3 (cat, cater, category)
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount('ca'));   // 3
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount('c'));    // 3
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount('dog'));  // 1
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount('z'));    // 0 (non-existent prefix)
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount(''));     // 0 (empty string)
console.log(trie.getPrefixCount(null));   // 0 (null input)
```

## Complexity Analysis

- **Time Complexity**: O(m) for insert, O(k) for getPrefixCount where m is word length and k is prefix length
- **Space Complexity**: O(ALPHABET_SIZE × m × n) where m is average word length and n is number of words, but optimized with Map-based children

